#
# THE DELETION IS THE HEADLINE. `scripts/check-doc-growth.py` goes on owner
# ruling: its per-file byte ceilings cannot be expressed as baseline rows,
# because a row for a file UNDER its class is dropped as stale and `*.md` is a
# frozen class no repo entry can override. Its two mentions in KEN-938's ledger
# above now name a file that no longer exists, and they stay — the ledger records
# what a past branch spent, and rewriting it to hide a later deletion would make
# the arithmetic unauditable.
#
# THE DELETION TOOK THE LAST HISTORICAL_SECTIONS ENTRY WITH IT, which is the two
# section-pointer raises. An empty table drives neither staleness direction, so
# both control scripts would have gone quiet on a rule that still ships. They
# install a fixture entry instead — in-process in the unit control, patched into
# the guard the e2e copies into a throwaway repo — and go back to driving the
# shipped entries the day this repo records another removed section. A rule
# survives its last real instance only if something stands in for it, and the
# stand-in has to be built in both places the rule is exercised.
#
# THE OTHER FOUR ARE ONE LANE, AND THREE REVIEW ROUNDS ON IT. The guard claimed
# the scripts/validate manifest and ci.yml move in lockstep and compared only
# executable `scripts/*` entries, so the two rendered engines this branch wired
# in — size-ratchet and todo-ban, both under `.agents/` — were compared by
# nothing, and deleting either workflow step left the inventory green. Closing
# that was ten lines. The rest is what three rounds of review asked for, and
# each round found the previous repair still fail-OPEN:
#
#   round 1   `path in ci_text` is satisfied by a MENTION — `echo <path>`, or a
#             trailing comment ci_run_commands' whole-line filter does not reach
#   round 2   splitting a line on separator CHARACTERS cannot see quoting, so
#             `echo "( <path> )"` made the path the first word of a segment
#   round 3   a redirection is not a separator (`: > <path>` truncates the file
#             and runs nothing) and `<path>() { :; }` DEFINES a name rather than
#             calling it
#   round 4   a path can be a real command that never runs — the right side of
#             `||`, and the body of an `if`, `while` or `case`
#   round 5   `name=(<path>)` is an array ELEMENT, and `(` there is not a
#             command separator
#   round 6   a heredoc closes on a line that is EXACTLY the delimiter; a
#             trimmed comparison closed it early and read the body as commands
#   round 7   DECLINED, and the first one that is. `false && <path> || true`
#             does skip the path, and the predicate does count it — the finding
#             is accurate, and a command after `&&` is counted as covered even
#             where a later `||` recovers the list. It does not converge:
#             telling that chain from `cd x && <path>` needs the left side's
#             EXIT STATUS, which is runtime. The reviewer's example is decidable
#             only because its left side is the literal `false`, and a rule
#             keyed on that literal buys one spelling. Same open class as
#             command substitution, `eval`, aliases and sourced files. The
#             boundary is corrected to name the worst case rather than widened
#             to half-model it, and this is the last patch round on this
#             predicate: a further lexical-shape finding is declined against it.
#
# So the predicate tokenizes with shlex, quote-aware, distinguishes separators
# from redirections, consumes redirection operands, skips heredoc bodies, joins
# backslash continuations, declines a definition, and excludes short-circuit and
# conditional branches. The first five are places a path is not a command at
# all; the last is a place it is a command that may never run. Every one was a
# false green until a reviewer named it. Seven fixture cases now, one per shape,
# and each new case goes red against the implementation the round before it
# while the older cases stay green — which is what makes each a control on ITS
# finding rather than on the lane in general.
#
# WHERE IT STOPS, and this is the last entry that widens it. The predicate
# APPROXIMATES shell execution and cannot decide it; the docstring on ci_runs
# now names what it models and what it never will — AND-OR recovery, command
# substitution, `eval`, aliases, sourced files, expansions, and anything
# depending on a runtime value — plus the step-level `if:` it cannot see, which
# four lanes in
# this repo already use as their real wiring. Six rounds each named a genuine
# construct and each repair was right, which is the argument FOR writing the
# limit down rather than against it. A proof that a lane ran needs CI declaring
# what it executed; a seventh construct is a reason to build that, not to widen
# this.
#
# That ratio, one lane's predicate against four raised rows, is this header's
# recurring argument arriving again: the fix is small every time and the cost is
# the controls that stop it being wrong a fourth way.
#
